TEST 28863 -- MRP - Stack failover master/slave
===========================================================================
Run: 2026-09-30 13:42-14:25 NZDT, tb470, hand-driven (no framework script; the
case has no steps in ck.db -- num_steps 1, empty). Tester: bench-runner, queue row 15.
Case scope (Terrence, 2026-09-30, verbatim): "please do a basic stack failover check
while MRP is enabled and passing traffic, that will suffice."

VERDICT: PASS -- graded against the TITLE ("MRP - Stack failover master/slave"),
labelled CLEAN. Re-graded 2026-09-30 from the FAIL of this run (same evidence,
no re-run).

  RE-GRADE (Terrence, 2026-09-30, relayed by the sentinel, verbatim): "re-grade.
  what does the test title want you to do?" The title asks whether MRP survives a
  stack failover of the master and of a slave. It does, for both:
    - traffic continues across the failover: worst unicast outage 196 ms, worst
      broadcast outage 20 ms, and all four flows recover (runs A, B, C = master; D =
      backup);
    - the MRP config and roles carry over to the new master: Manager, Closed, same
      primary/secondary;
    - the ring closes again, and the MRC is unaffected;
    - the stack re-forms: 1/3/4 Ready, Normal operation, one build
      awplus_main-20260923-20 on every member.

  OBSERVATION (kept; it was this run's former FAIL condition): a stack MASTER
  failover with MRP running makes the stack's MRM declare a physically intact
  ring OPEN and unblock its secondary port, so the ring LOOPS for a few hundred ms.
  Broadcast frames were received up to 264 times each, and each host NIC received
  its OWN broadcasts back (runs A and C). The same false "Ring opened" fires when a
  member REJOINS the stack, even a backup member (runs D and B). Loop test: the
  same frame arriving more than once, and frames coming back to the NIC that sent
  them; neither happens in the baseline. The mechanism is the MRM's false
  `CHK_RC -> CHK_RO evt=MRP_ProtEvTestTimerExpired`. Row 9 then showed it fires on
  EVERY ring closure, with no stack event, on either IE520 as MRM. That defect is
  graded where ring closure is the subject: T38093 FAIL
  (38093-fail.log); see also 38098.log, 38099.log (at the 500 ms profile only 1
  of 8 closures looped) and 38097.log.

